Early FINISH (GOW_GS_EARLY_FINISH=1, opt-in; the launcher turns it on by default with a switch to disable it).
The game's FINISH arrives in a 32-byte PATH2 (VIF1 DIRECT) packet at the end of each frame's VIF1 chain; the EE then
waited for the whole VU1 job before preparing the next frame, leaving the worker idle ~4-8 ms per frame (measured with
PS2X_GS_WORKER_TRACE: 26.9 ms job + 4.85 ms median serial gap in combat). Now the EE scans each VIF1 packet at
submission for PATH2 A+D writes to FINISH (0x61) and raises FINISH at once when no other job waits ahead (else when the
worker starts the job), so the EE runs at most one job ahead. Stage markers make the GS skip that job's own FINISH
writes, cleared at job end so a miscount cannot leak into later jobs. CSR reads/acknowledges no longer wait for the
worker; display-register writes (gsDisplayStore) go through the worker queue and are applied by the GS stage in drawing
order, with a shadow copy for read-modify-write. Verified in the game code: 0x17A0E0 sets the busy flag before the kick
and the GS handler 0x182DD8 only clears it, so an early interrupt cannot be lost.
Tests: tools/test/dma_worker_tests.cpp (scan incl. decoy UNPACK data and a cut DIRECT, raise at submission, CSR and
display not blocked by a held stage, marker order). Measured (Xeon E5-2666 v3 / RX 580, scale 2, 3 alternating pairs,
same executable): mean 57.80 vs 54.63 FPS (+5.8%), combat 52.22 vs 43.45 FPS (+20%); four in-game window captures in
heavy combat correct. Test-only VRAM dumps (GOW_PAD_CAPTURAS) now catch the next frame mid-draw, as the EE runs ahead.

